echnical (weather, computers). Weakness: interpersonal (nasty remarks) and memory for numbers (important for calculations). Could be concerning.\n\nCandidate C: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, but regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. Strengths: situational awareness, technical cognition, leadership. Weakness: attitude, openness. Could affect crew cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, but regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Weakness: temperament, attitude.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Likely candidate C has strong technical and situational awareness, leadership, but attitude issues (show-off, not open). Candidate B has good stress and conscientiousness but interpersonal issues (nasty remarks). Candidate D has good problem solving but hot temper and know-it-all. Candidate A has decision speed and calm but poor communication and egocentric.\n\nWhich is best? Perhaps candidate C's strengths align with many criteria: anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership. Attitude concerns could be mitigated? Show-off may affect crew cooperation, not open to new ideas may affect adaptability. But other candidates have more severe interpersonal issues (nasty remarks, hot temper). So maybe C is best overall.\n\nThus initial recommendation: Candidate C.\n\nWe need to reference discussion (none) but we can say \"I propose we start by looking at Candidate C\". Provide evidence: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, leadership. Also note concerns: show-off, not open to new ideas. Explain why still best.\n\nLet's produce public message with vote C."}
